---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Env from '../components/Env.astro';
import Sources from '../components/Sources.astro';
import { blob } from '../lib/url';
import { bare, k8s, nf, rows, row, speedup, peakProcess } from '../lib/data';

const matrixSources = [k8s.sources[0], ...new Set(k8s.matrix.map((m) => m.source))];
const k0 = [row(0, 1), row(0, 10)];
const k50 = [row(50, 1), row(50, 10)];
const [kb, kf] = k8s.keda;
const lat = bare.latency[0];
---

<Base title="Performance" description="Interactive charts of DurableQ benchmarks on a bare-process setup and on a local kind cluster, each tied to the raw file it was read from, with the environment named.">
  <div class="page">
    <header>
      <h1>Performance</h1>
      <p class="lead">Measured, not estimated, on one Apple M3 Pro laptop. Two different setups produced these numbers, and they are kept apart on purpose: the figures from one are not comparable to the figures from the other. One run per cell; treat them as orders of magnitude and relative comparisons, not capacity planning.</p>
    </header>

    <section aria-labelledby="k8s-h">
      <h2 id="k8s-h">On Kubernetes <Env env="kind" /></h2>
      <p class="muted">{k8s.environment}. Workload <code>demo.effect</code>: sleeps the stated time, then writes one effect row in the completion transaction. KEDA was paused for the fixed-replica runs. Postgres runs inside a kind node; each worker pod has 6 connections.</p>

      <div class="chart-block">
        <h3>Throughput by replica count</h3>
        <div class="chart-meta"><Env env="kind" /><span>Source: <Sources paths={matrixSources} /></span></div>
        <label class="check"><input type="checkbox" id="show-ref" /> Show the linear-scaling reference (dashed, computed from the 1-replica rate, not measured)</label>
        <div class="chart" data-chart="throughput" role="img" aria-label="Line chart of jobs per second against worker replicas, for 0 ms and 50 ms of work per job. Exact values are in the table below."></div>
        <details>
          <summary>Data table</summary>
          <div class="table-wrap"><table>
            <thead><tr><th>Work per job</th><th class="num">Replicas</th><th class="num">Jobs</th><th class="num">Jobs/s</th><th class="num">× vs 1 replica</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {[0, 50].flatMap((ms) => rows(ms).map((m) => (
                <tr><td>{ms} ms</td><td class="num">{m.replicas}</td><td class="num">{nf(m.jobs)}</td><td class="num">{nf(m.jobs_per_second, 1)}</td><td class="num">{nf(m.jobs_per_second / rows(ms)[0].jobs_per_second, 1)}×</td></tr>
              )))}
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
        </details>
        <p class="caption">With 50 ms of work per job, ten replicas give {nf(speedup(50), 1)}× the throughput of one. With no work per job they give {nf(speedup(0), 1)}×.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="chart-block">
        <h3>Why it flattens: the database is the bottleneck</h3>
        <div class="chart-meta"><Env env="kind" /><span>Source: <Sources paths={matrixSources} /></span></div>
        <div class="chart-pair">
          <div>
            <p class="small"><strong>0 ms of work per job</strong></p>
            <div class="chart" data-chart="cpu-0" role="img" aria-label="Postgres and total worker CPU in millicores against worker replicas, 0 ms of work per job. Values are in the table below."></div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="small"><strong>50 ms of work per job</strong></p>
            <div class="chart" data-chart="cpu-50" role="img" aria-label="Postgres and total worker CPU in millicores against worker replicas, 50 ms of work per job. Values are in the table below."></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <details>
          <summary>Data table</summary>
          <div class="table-wrap"><table>
            <thead><tr><th>Work per job</th><th class="num">Replicas</th><th class="num">Postgres CPU (m)</th><th class="num">Worker CPU, summed (m)</th><th class="num">Peak PG connections</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {[0, 50].flatMap((ms) => rows(ms).map((m) => (
                <tr><td>{ms} ms</td><td class="num">{m.replicas}</td><td class="num">{nf(m.postgres_cpu_millicores)}</td><td class="num">{nf(m.worker_cpu_millicores)}</td><td class="num">{m.peak_postgres_connections}</td></tr>
              )))}
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
        </details>
        <p class="caption">CPU values are metrics-server window averages (about 15 s), sampled about every 2 s, summed across worker pods at the same moment. They are not instantaneous peaks.</p>
        <p>At 0 ms of work, ten replicas drive Postgres to {nf(k0[1].postgres_cpu_millicores / 1000, 1)} of the VM’s 11 cores while all workers together use {nf(k0[1].worker_cpu_millicores / 1000, 2)} of one core. Each job costs a claim, an effect insert and a fenced completion transaction, so when handlers do no work the database is the limit and more workers mostly add contention: {nf(speedup(0), 1)}×. With 50 ms of work the database spends more time waiting, and the same ten replicas reach {nf(speedup(50), 1)}×, with Postgres at {nf(k50[1].postgres_cpu_millicores / 1000, 1)} cores. Nothing beyond 10 replicas was measured, so where Postgres saturates with 50 ms of work is unknown.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="chart-block">
        <h3>Processing time and queue wait</h3>
        <div class="chart-meta"><Env env="kind" /><span>Source: <Sources paths={matrixSources} /></span></div>
        <fieldset>
          <legend>Work per job</legend>
          <div class="seg">
            <label><input type="radio" name="work" value="0" checked /><span>0 ms</span></label>
            <label><input type="radio" name="work" value="50" /><span>50 ms</span></label>
          </div>
        </fieldset>
        <div class="chart-pair">
          <div>
            <p class="small"><strong>Processing time</strong> (milliseconds)</p>
            <div class="chart" data-chart="processing" role="img" aria-label="Processing time p50, p95 and p99 in milliseconds by replica count. Values are in the table below."></div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="small"><strong>Queue wait</strong> (seconds)</p>
            <div class="chart" data-chart="queue-wait" role="img" aria-label="Queue wait p50, p95 and p99 in seconds by replica count. Values are in the table below."></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <details>
          <summary>Data table</summary>
          <div class="table-wrap"><table>
            <thead><tr><th>Work</th><th class="num">Replicas</th><th class="num">Processing p50 / p95 / p99 (ms)</th><th class="num">Queue wait p50 / p95 / p99 (s)</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {[0, 50].flatMap((ms) => rows(ms).map((m) => (
                <tr><td>{ms} ms</td><td class="num">{m.replicas}</td><td class="num">{m.processing_ms.p50} / {m.processing_ms.p95} / {m.processing_ms.p99}</td><td class="num">{m.queue_wait_s.p50} / {m.queue_wait_s.p95} / {m.queue_wait_s.p99}</td></tr>
              )))}
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
        </details>
        <p class="caption">Queue wait here mostly measures time spent behind the up-front backlog (the whole batch is due at once), not service latency. Processing time is claim to attempt outcome.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="chart-block">
        <h3>Recovery from a SIGKILLed worker (30 s lease)</h3>
        <div class="chart-meta"><Env env="kind" /><span>Source: <Sources paths={[k8s.recovery.source]} /></span></div>
        <div class="chart short" data-chart="recovery" role="img" aria-label={`Range of recovery times for ${k8s.recovery.claims} claims: ${k8s.recovery.min_s} to ${k8s.recovery.max_s} seconds, against a 30 second lease.`}></div>
        <p class="caption">A worker holding {k8s.recovery.claims} claims was SIGKILLed; each job was claimed again {nf(k8s.recovery.min_s, 2)} to {nf(k8s.recovery.max_s, 2)} s later. A killed worker cannot release its jobs, so recovery takes one lease. A shorter lease recovers faster at the cost of more frequent heartbeats. The axis spans 28 to 31 s so the {nf(k8s.recovery.max_s - k8s.recovery.min_s, 2)} s spread is visible. This measurement was not rerun on the final build.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="chart-block">
        <h3>KEDA scaling response</h3>
        <div class="chart-meta"><Env env="kind" /><span>Source: <Sources paths={[...new Set(k8s.keda.map((k) => k.source))]} /></span></div>
        <div class="chart short" style="height: 20rem" data-chart="keda" role="img" aria-label="Seconds to first scale-up decision, to ten ready workers and back to three workers, for two runs. Values are in the table below."></div>
        <details>
          <summary>Data table</summary>
          <div class="table-wrap"><table>
            <thead><tr><th>Run</th><th>Commit</th><th class="num">First scale-up (s)</th><th class="num">10 workers ready (s)</th><th class="num">Back to 3 (s)</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {k8s.keda.map((k) => (
                <tr><td>{k.run}</td><td><code>{k.commit}</code></td><td class="num">{k.first_scale_up_s}</td><td class="num">{k.ten_ready_s}</td><td class="num">{k.back_to_min_s}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
        </details>
        <p>The burst’s jobs become due 5 s after enqueue, and both scale-up times include that delay. The scale-up delay is KEDA’s 15 s polling plus pod start-up, and it varies between runs by up to one polling interval.</p>
        <p><strong>Why scale-down differs.</strong> The benchmark run used a chart with only the 120 s stabilization window, after which the HPA dropped from 10 to 3 replicas in one step ({kb.back_to_min_s} s). The final chart also sheds at most {kf.scale_down_limit_pods_per_min} pods per minute, so 10 → 3 takes the window plus about 3.5 minutes ({kf.back_to_min_s} s). The slower scale-down is deliberate: it keeps the cluster from shedding workers that are still finishing jobs. See <a href={blob('docs/kubernetes.md#autoscaling-keda')}>the autoscaling notes</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="bare-h">
      <h2 id="bare-h">On bare processes <Env env="process" /></h2>
      <p class="muted">{bare.environment}. Worker processes are separate OS processes, <code>--poll 100ms</code>, with Postgres on the same machine and default settings (<code>synchronous_commit=on</code>). Each job costs at least two committed transactions.</p>

      <div class="chart-block">
        <h3>Claim throughput sweep</h3>
        <div class="chart-meta"><Env env="process" /><span>Source: <Sources paths={[bare.sources[0]]} /></span></div>
        <div class="chart" data-chart="sweep" role="img" aria-label="Jobs per second for 1, 2, 4 and 8 worker processes at 1, 10 and 50 slots per process. Values are in the table below."></div>
        <details>
          <summary>Data table</summary>
          <div class="table-wrap"><table>
            <thead><tr><th class="num">Processes</th><th class="num">Slots each</th><th class="num">Pool each</th><th class="num">Jobs/s</th><th class="num">Peak worker connections</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {bare.throughput.map((t) => (
                <tr><td class="num">{t.procs}</td><td class="num">{t.concurrency}</td><td class="num">{t.pool_per_proc}</td><td class="num">{nf(t.jobs_per_sec, 1)}</td><td class="num">{t.max_worker_conns}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
        </details>
        <p class="caption">A pre-enqueued backlog of {nf(peakProcess.jobs)} <code>demo.noop</code> jobs; throughput is measured on the database clock. It plateaus around 10 to 11 thousand jobs/s from 200 concurrent jobs upward, at an 80-connection budget: {peakProcess.procs} × {peakProcess.concurrency} reached {nf(peakProcess.jobs_per_sec)} jobs/s.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="row">
        <div>
          <h3>Latency and recovery</h3>
          <div class="table-wrap"><table>
            <thead><tr><th>Measurement</th><th>Setup</th><th class="num">Result</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><th scope="row">Queue latency, first attempts</th><td>{lat.rate_per_sec} jobs/s open-loop for 10 s ({nf(lat.jobs)} jobs), 4 processes × 10, poll 100 ms</td><td class="num">p50 {lat.p50_ms} ms<br />p95 {lat.p95_ms} ms<br />p99 {lat.p99_ms} ms</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Recovery after SIGKILL</th><td>{bare.recovery.jobs} long jobs held by one worker, lease {bare.recovery.lease}, heartbeat 500 ms</td><td class="num">p50 {nf(bare.recovery.p50_ms, 1)} ms<br />max {nf(bare.recovery.max_ms, 1)} ms</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p class="caption">Polling sets the latency: an idle worker sleeps <code>poll ± 20%</code>. Recovery lands just under one lease. Sources: <Sources paths={[bare.sources[1], bare.sources[2]]} />.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <h2>Limits of these numbers</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>One laptop, one dev Postgres, one run per cell, no confidence intervals. Postgres and workers share CPU.</li>
      <li>The bare-process figures (8 × 50 slots, 80 connections, host Postgres) and the kind figures (6 connections per pod, Postgres in a kind node on a Docker VM) describe different setups and are not comparable.</li>
      <li>Nothing here is production capacity. Full methodology: <a href={blob('docs/results.md')}>results.md</a> and <a href={blob('docs/results-k8s.md')}>results-k8s.md</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</Base>

<script>
  import '../scripts/charts';
</script>
